\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Concept Embedding Model}: from an intermediate latent code $h$, we learn two embeddings per concept, one for when it is active (i.e., $\hat  {c}^+_i$), and another when it is inactive (i.e., $\hat  {c}^-_i$). Each concept embedding (shown in this example as a vector with $m = 2$ activations) is then aligned to its corresponding ground truth concept through the scoring function $s(\cdot )$, which learns to assign activation probabilities $\hat  {p}_i$ for each concept. These probabilities are used to output an embedding for each concept via a weighted mixture of each concept's positive and negative embedding.}}{4}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:f3}{{3}{4}{\textbf {Concept Embedding Model}: from an intermediate latent code $h$, we learn two embeddings per concept, one for when it is active (i.e., $\hat {c}^+_i$), and another when it is inactive (i.e., $\hat {c}^-_i$). Each concept embedding (shown in this example as a vector with $m = 2$ activations) is then aligned to its corresponding ground truth concept through the scoring function $s(\cdot )$, which learns to assign activation probabilities $\hat {p}_i$ for each concept. These probabilities are used to output an embedding for each concept via a weighted mixture of each concept's positive and negative embedding}{figure.3}{}}
